In the late game, sometimes you have to uncurse just one item, and
you need to do it right now, not wait until you have several other
cursed items to uncurse with it.  Examples abound:  the Wizard of
Yendor cursed your shield of reflection, and you don't dare wait
to uncurse it because if you do he will of course curse your sword
also.  Conversely, one of the priests on the astral plane cursed
your artifact sword, and you need to uncurse it before anything
unfortunate happens to your shield of reflection.  Somebody cursed
your off-hand saber, and you want your silver damage back.  An
invisible golden naga took your bag of holding from blessed straight
to cursed and you can hardly move.  Your unicorn horn got cursed and
you don't dare leave it that way because you're about to face down
Pestilence (although, as noted earlier, a really dedicated
overpreparer
carries at least two blessed and positively enchanted unicorn
horns).
Your oilskin cloak became cursed and you don't want to risk leaving
it that way on the plane of water, because you didn't nuke ; from
orbit.  Death cursed your stack of lembas wafers and now you're two
tiles away from Famine.  (Yes, you should've kept most of the food in
the bag, but we all make occasional stupid mistakes, don't we?)
Some of these don't apply to the O.P., but on the other hand this
list of specific examples is very far from exhaustive.

In situations like that, wherein you only need to uncurse one thing,
the holy water is usually the way to go, because potions are more
abundant than magic marker charges.  (However, this dynamic can
change if you have the resources to do really extensive polypiling
of scrolls -- potions are more abundant than marker charges, but
scrolls are more abundant, and uses for them less frequent, than is
the case for potions.)

Again, I'm not criticizing the O.P. -- he ascended, so obviously the
preparation he did was adequate for his situation.  I'm responding
to the guy who criticized him for overpreparing.  I'm not sure he
knows the true meaning of the word "overprepare".  Because, this
game?  This is not a particularly good example of overpreparation.


